CS2-c023 L02 T |F: Is hashing O(1) always? |B: No. Corollary 2.3 bounds the average cost only under Assumption 2.1 with n <= c m. Proposition 1.4 gives a key set on which an unsuccessful search makes n probes. |basis: None
CS2-c033 L03 T |F: In the same hold run, what bounds the sorted list's total moves? |B: Assume the hold run of Lab 3 with N events, N initial pushes, N hold steps and N drain pops, and that the list never holds more than N keys. The sorted list makes at most 2N(N + 1) moves in total (Proposition 1.6). |basis: None
CS2-c037 L03 T |F: Is a pop O(1) because it removes only one key? |B: No. Assume distinct keys in the array shape of Definition 1.1 with s >= 1 keys. The pop restores heap order by a sift-down, which makes up to 2 floor(log2 s) comparisons (Proposition 1.3(b)). That bound grows with s, and no constant bound on a pop is proved. |basis: None
CS2-c063 L05 C |F: Does a rotation restore the AVL rule after every insertion? |B: Not proved here. Proposition 3.1 shows only that a rotation keeps the in-order sequence. Lab 1, Part A checks the rule after each of 500 seeded insertions, and that check is the only evidence for it (Remark 3.2). |basis: Lab 1, Part A (one seeded run of 500 insertions; checked, not proved)
CS2-c091 L07 T |F: How much can lambda change in one step of the LCS table? |B: Assume x and y are finite sequences compared by exact equality. Then lambda(i - 1, j) <= lambda(i, j) <= lambda(i - 1, j) + 1, and lambda(i, j - 1) <= lambda(i, j) <= lambda(i, j - 1) + 1 (Lemma 3.2). |basis: None
CS2-c096 L07 T |F: Is dynamic programming cheap in the input size, so the coin table is always cheap? |B: No. Assume A >= 1 is an integer. The coin table has A + 1 entries, which is exponential in the b bits that write A (Proposition 4.2). An amount of 2^40 needs 41 bits and more than 10^12 entries. |basis: None
CS2-c102 L08 C |F: How are prices and quantities stored in the order book? |B: Prices are integer cents, so 10000 is 100.00 currency units and one tick is one cent. Quantities are integer shares. The code raises an error for any other type, and Part A shows a float price being refused (Section 1). |basis: Lab 1, Part A (printed line: part A: a float price is refused)
CS2-c112 L08 T |F: Can a bid above the best ask rest in the book? |B: No. Assume a book that starts empty, with each message a submission matched by Definition 2.1 or a cancellation of a live order. An incoming buy above the best ask trades first, and only its unfilled remainder rests, as a bid at its own price, below the best ask (Proposition 1.3). |basis: None
CS2-c125 L09 C |F: Does the sum-of-squares formula give a correct algorithm at any scale, because it is an exact identity? |B: No. Lemma 1.3 is an identity for real numbers, but the naive algorithm computes it in floating point, where at offset 10^9 the rounding of its two running sums is larger than the quantity they estimate. |basis: Lab 1, offset 10^9 (SIMULATED stream, one seed)
CS2-c133 L10 T |F: What is the average number of operations per insertion over a run of n values? |B: Assume w >= 1 is fixed, n >= 1 values are inserted at positions 0 to n - 1, and the values are real with exact comparisons (Corollary 2.5). At most 2 per insertion on average, so the deque has amortized O(1) cost per value, where amortized means the average over a run of insertions. Corollary 2.5(a) gives that average, and Corollary 2.5(c) gives the single-insertion caveat. |basis: None
CS2-c137 L10 T |F: Is each push constant time in the worst case, so the deque is constant time per value? |B: No. Assume w >= 1 is fixed, n >= 1 values are inserted at positions 0 to n - 1, and the values are real with exact comparisons. One push can then remove w entries (Corollary 2.5(c)). |basis: None
CS2-c153 L11 T |F: Does every stored item count once in the quantile answer? |B: No. An item at level h has weight 2^h (Definition 2.1), and the query adds the weights. Assume the values are added in order, epsilon is fixed in (0, 1) and arithmetic is exact. Then the weights sum to n (Theorem 2.2(a)). |basis: None
CS2-c165 L01 T |F: What is the first integer n >= 1 with n^2 > 1000 n? |B: n = 1001, the value Lab 1 prints. Remark 2.2 says the bound n^2 <= 1000 n holds for n <= 1000 and fails from n = 1001. |basis: None
CS2-c195 L09 C |F: What does Welford's estimate return at offset 10^9 in Lab 1? |B: 8.728109e-02, which rounds to the exact sample variance 0.087281 at six digits (one stream and one seed). |basis: Lab 1 (SIMULATED stream, one seed), offset 1e9 lines
